WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.600
The database has been built, and some data&nbsp;
has been entered. Now let’s retrieve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.920
information we will need to build reports. We do&nbsp;
this by building queries, questions we ask of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.840
information. As we go through this domain, try&nbsp;
to understand when you would use each keyword in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.240
building queries. The majority of queries one will&nbsp;
run in a database will use two or more tables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.360
The whole purpose of having a relational database&nbsp;
is to store data efficiently across tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.480
and then bring data from those tables together&nbsp;
using queries. For this example, we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:41.520
to build a query that retrieves information from&nbsp;
both the Sales.Customer and Sales.SalesOrderHeader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.640
tables. I have a SELECT statement started that&nbsp;
has columns from both these tables, and so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.840
the FROM line indicates that we are just&nbsp;
selecting data from the Sales.Customer table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.680
To add the Sales.SalesOrderHeader table,&nbsp;
we need a JOIN statement. I will type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:07.560
JOIN Sales.SalesOrderHeader&nbsp;
on Sales.Customer.CustomerID =&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:24.800
Sales.SalesOrderHeader.CustomerID as the&nbsp;
field used to relate these two tables is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.840
CustomerID. CustomerID is the primary&nbsp;
key in the Sales.Customer table and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:39.360
a foreign key in the Sales.SalesOrderHeader&nbsp;
table. Before running this, here is a fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:46.480
common problem with a join: The CustomerID and&nbsp;
AccountNumber columns both have red squiggly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.920
lines under them. When hovering the mouse over&nbsp;
each, we see that the column name is ambiguous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.520
meaning it is in both tables. In these&nbsp;
situations, we need to specify which table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:07.840
we want to represent for each column. I will add&nbsp;
Sales.Customer. in front of both of these columns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:22.240
I will now execute this, and we have just over&nbsp;
31000 records involving customers and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:30.160
orders. We just ran what is called an inner join,&nbsp;
which is a join in which records will only display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.960
if the value of the join field is in both tables.&nbsp;
To put this an easier way, as the CustomerID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.760
field is the one used to join these two tables,&nbsp;
records will only show if the customer ID appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.880
in both tables. However, what if we want to see&nbsp;
all of the customers from the customer table?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.200
For that, we need an outer join, and there&nbsp;
are three types of outer joins we can use:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.640
left, right, and full outer joins. To determine&nbsp;
what join we should use, we first want to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.000
out the table with all the records we want to&nbsp;
see. In this case, it would be Sales.Customer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.920
Secondly, which table is left and which is&nbsp;
right? Between the two tables used in a join,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.640
the table on the left is listed first.&nbsp;
That would be Sales.Customer in this case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.520
Thus, we want a left outer join.&nbsp;
I will add the words LEFT OUTER&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:35.360
to the join and notice that we have about 700&nbsp;
more records than before when executing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.880
indicating that we have 700 or so customers who&nbsp;
have never placed an order. To see the other joins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.880
working, I will change this join from a left outer&nbsp;
join to a right outer join and execute this again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.880
The number of records reverts to the same number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:04:03.440
as the inner join, which is good because the&nbsp;
right table is this Sales.SalesOrderHeader table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.600
Should there be any customer IDs&nbsp;
from this table if they do not exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.080
in the Sales.Customer table? The answer is&nbsp;
no. Though not all customers place orders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.240
customers should exist before they place orders.&nbsp;
If you need to see both customers that have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.040
placed orders and customers that have sales&nbsp;
but no definition in the Sales.Customer table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:31.120
you can use a full outer join, as I am building&nbsp;
here. Normally you would only use this after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.480
combining two datasets into one, and you want to&nbsp;
see mismatched records from both tables in a join.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:45.440
I will execute this, and we are back to showing&nbsp;
all customers, including ones with no orders.

